description:
WHY: The epic acceptance requires that 'splitting a host wall keeps the door on the right part (WAL-030 hook)', and the epic risk names the interaction with the junction engine at wall ends, to be covered by door fixtures at L and T corners in the NFR-MNT-005 regression suite. This task proves several requirements on realistic cases:
- DW-001: hosting through a split;
- DW-002: host cycling at a T junction;
- DW-009: the cut of all skins next to a junction;
- DW-018: export after the split.
junction-engine owns the suite (crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/junctions.rs and docs/junctions.md) and drafting-trim-split owns Command::Split; neither is a prerequisite of this epic.

WHAT: Seeded fixtures built with the FixtureBuilder of element-kind-framework:
- dw_l_corner: an F2 L corner with a door 0.10 m from the inner corner;
- dw_t_junction: an F2 through-wall with a 0.25 m wall abutting it and a door next to the abutment;
- dw_near_junction_window: a window whose interval reaches into the junction zone, which must produce the 03 warning.
Register them in the case table of crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/junctions.rs, and in the 'openings at element boundaries' row of docs/junctions.md, if junction-engine has merged. Otherwise keep them in crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/inserts.rs and file a bead to move them. Also add them to the D.7-style fixture registry crates/archicraft-model/tests/fixtures.rs if regression-and-status created it. Each fixture asserts closed per-skin bodies, the expected volumes and an unchanged junction footprint outside the void.

Split check on samples::ground_floor_with_openings(): use Command::Split {targets, cutter: Cutter::Point(..), id_seed} (drafting-trim-split-10) if present, otherwise relations::retarget_after_replace or kinds::insert::rehost_inserts directly. A door left of the split point stays on the first part, a door right of it moves to the second part with the same absolute position, and the IFCX export reflects the new parents. Out of scope: the scripted scenario and the viewer check (12), the full AS-01 model (reference-models).

design:
Checklist-driven: map each acceptance item to the test or document that proves it:
- model tests in crates/archicraft-model/tests/inserts.rs (01, 02);
- geometry tests in crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/inserts.rs (03, 04);
- IFCX tests, the golden file and check.ts (05, 06);
- kittest in crates/archicraft-app/tests/ui.rs and unit tests in tools/insert.rs (07, 08, 09, 10);
- the junction fixtures and the split check (11);
- crates/archicraft-app/tests/ground_floor_precision.rs, docs/test-plan/increment-1-ground-floor.md and the AS-01 viewer record (12).
Record the mapping in the status document's evidence column. Re-run the scenario script on a second platform if one is available (AS acceptance is per platform).

description:
WHY: A placed door or window must look like the configured unit. The exterior side is shown and reversible (DW-003). The opening configuration (hinge left/right x opening towards either side, or the sliding direction) is visible in plan and 3D (DW-004). The empty opening shows no frame and no leaf (DW-010). The material parts written by DW-018 (frame, leaf or sash, glazing, sill, each with its material) must exist as geometry. VIEW-055's 'schematic or detailed 3D representation' needs both representations available per unit. components-dw-catalogue delivers the pieces in unit-local coordinates:
- the unit generators at two DetailLevels, where Simplified is what VIEW-055 calls schematic (04, 05);
- the plan SymbolPrimitive output (06);
- shared type geometry: components::evaluate_instance, InstanceGeometry, the TypeCache and PlanElement.symbol (07);
- painting of symbols in plan (10).
This task places them in the wall.

WHAT: The door and window GeometryKind modules call components::evaluate_instance(ctx, &frame.effective_instance(), frame.unit_to_storey) (components-dw-catalogue-07). The resulting ElementGeometry carries an InstanceGeometry whose transform is InsertFrame's proper rotation plus translation, and keeps the Void part of task 03. Hinge end, swing side and exterior side reach the generator only through instance.mirrored and the swing and opening-side parameters, so no negative-determinant transform arises (IFX-051), and identical units share one Arc<TypeGeometry> from the TypeCache. Both DetailLevels must be reachable per element: if world_parts() has no detail argument, add world_parts_at(DetailLevel), with world_parts() meaning Detailed, for render3d (task 10).

Plan:
- the placed SymbolPrimitive list of PlanElement.symbol is reclassified per unit: Cut when the storey's cut plane lies within [sill, head], Overhead when the unit lies entirely above it, Projected when it lies below;
- the catalogue's Mask {0, width} is extended across the host thickness (components-dw-catalogue-06 leaves this step to this task);
- verify that PlanElement::segments(), hit() and pick() (crates/archicraft-geometry/src/plan.rs:211-270) and the hover path of components-dw-catalogue-10 include insert symbols, so a door can be picked by its leaf and arc.
Out of scope: the per-view filter (10), IFCX (05), painting (components-dw-catalogue-10).

WHY: Several placement choices must stay editable after placement without disturbing the others:
- DW-003: the exterior side 'shall be reversible later without changing position, size or opening configuration';
- DW-004: hinge side, opening side and sliding direction 'can each be changed independently afterwards';
- DW-005: the anchor stays fixed when the width changes;
- DW-006: width and height apply to the selection, and the element updates immediately;
- DW-007: the reference storey may differ from the host's storey and can be reassigned, and the epic acceptance requires that sill and head references to any storey follow storey changes.
DW-001 also requires that units move with their host and are deleted with it. host-openings-02 provides the generic cascade: hosted_closure on delete, Kind::carry on move, rehome and relink_to_storey carrying hosted elements, and copy::duplicate. Host edits that are not rigid moves still need rules specific to inserts:
- dragging a wall end along its line;
- FlipWalls and MoveWallReferenceLine, since layer-structures-06 leaves re-anchoring hosted inserts to the hosted kinds;
- a STR-004 storey move that keeps absolute positions;
- WAL-030's split, for which drafting-trim-split-10 prepares the descriptor hook rehost(&hosted, &parts) and names kinds::insert::rehost_inserts as the door implementation.
Today MoveElements (crates/archicraft-model/src/command.rs:153), rehome and relink_to_storey (command.rs:399-432) and plan_delete_storey (command.rs:435) know nothing about hosted elements.

WHAT: New commands in crates/archicraft-model/src/command.rs, each with a label(), a plan() into ReplaceElement edits, and NoChange/Locked/Invalid handling:
- FlipInsertSide {ids};
- SetInsertConfiguration {ids, hinge: Option, swing: Option, slide: Option};
- ResizeInserts {ids, width: Option, height: Option};
- SetInsertAnchor {ids, anchor}, which keeps the interval;
- SetInsertVertical {ids, edge: Option, reference: Option, offset: Option, keep_elevation: bool};
- SetInsertReveal {ids, reveal, keep_position: bool}.

Storey rules use the Kind hooks of element-kind-framework:
- storey_links() reports a VerticalReference::Storey, so the relation store's storey_dependents and validate_storey_change cover it;
- unlink_storey turns a reference to a deleted storey into HomeStorey, with the offset recomputed to keep the absolute elevation, and plan_delete_storey reports the conversion;
- rehome recomputes HomeStorey offsets, so a host moved to another storey with STR-004's 'keep absolute position' keeps the unit's elevations; element.storey keeps mirroring the host.

Host-change hooks are registered through the relation store and run before validation in the same plan:
- a host edit that keeps the reference line collinear (a wall end dragged, MoveWallReferenceLine) re-derives 'along', so each unit keeps its absolute anchor point;
- a host edit that reverses the reference line (FlipWalls) maps the host-axis choices (anchor, hinge end, slide direction) to their opposites and re-derives 'along'. With keep_body = true it also swaps WallSide and swing side, so the unit stays physically in place; with keep_body = false the unit follows the mirrored body;
- if a unit no longer fits, the whole edit is rejected with a message naming the unit;
- kinds::insert::rehost_inserts(&hosted, &parts) implements the rehost hook of drafting-trim-split-10, used by the WAL-030 split and the WAL-031 merge. It assigns each unit to the part that contains its whole interval and re-bases 'along'. A split point strictly inside a unit's interval returns an error, so the split is rejected.

SetInstanceMirrored and SetInstanceParameters (components-dw-catalogue-02) on doors or windows are routed to SetInsertConfiguration (hinge end) and ResizeInserts (the bound width and height), so the catalogue inspector cannot desynchronise the placement. Out of scope: UI (07-09) and geometry (03).

WHY: Two epic acceptance items need an end-to-end proof that no unit test gives: 'a ground floor with openings is drawn precisely end to end (increment-1 exit scenario scripted under docs/)' and 'the AS-01 walls show openings check passes in the viewer'. Appendix F's increment-1 exit criterion is 'a ground floor with openings can be drawn precisely'. SRS 7.1 verifies interaction-heavy requirements with scripted manual tests, AS-04 fixes the display precision at 0.1 mm and 0.01 degrees, and AS-01 passes when 'walls show openings and clean L/T junctions' in the IFC5 viewer. The scenario exercises the user-facing clauses together:
- DW-001: no host, no element;
- DW-002 and DW-003: host cycling and exterior side;
- DW-004 and DW-005: configuration and anchors;
- DW-006, DW-007 and DW-008: width and height, storey references, reveals;
- DW-010: the empty opening;
- DW-018: export;
- VIEW-055: hiding and simplifying units in 3D.

WHAT: (1) An automated companion, crates/archicraft-app/tests/ground_floor_precision.rs. It drives PointInput headlessly, as as04_precision.rs of point-input-snapping-15 does, with the wall tool and the door and window tools by typed values only, and rebuilds the ground floor of samples::ground_floor_with_openings(). It then compares every wall end point and every door and window placement (host, along, sill and head, reveal, exterior side, configuration) with the sample within MODEL_TOLERANCE, and formats them at 0.1 mm / 0.01 degrees.

(2) docs/test-plan/increment-1-ground-floor.md, a scripted manual procedure:
- a new project from the default template;
- the F2 exterior ring by typed polar coordinates, then the interior walls;
- a click without a wall (feedback shown, nothing created), then doors and windows at typed distances from corners;
- host cycling at the T, a configuration choice, a flip, a resize with each anchor, a reveal change to a core face, a storey elevation change with a window head referencing the upper storey, and hiding and simplifying units in 3D;
- saving as IFCX and opening the file in the reference viewer;
- an expected-values table (measured distances, sill and head elevations, wall volumes) and pass/fail boxes.
Execute it once on one platform and record the results in the document.

(3) AS-01 'walls show openings' on crates/archicraft-ifcx/tests/golden/ground-floor-openings.ifcx: run the headless viewer job of regression-and-status if it exists; otherwise record a manual viewer check with a screenshot in the scenario document (IFX-006 b).

Out of scope: the full AS-01 model (reference-models) and other platforms (the epic's done task re-runs the script if a second platform is available).

WHY: DW-006: 'Width and height shall be numeric parameters of the wall opening. They apply to new elements when nothing is selected and to the selection otherwise, and the element and the void shall update immediately.' The other placement axes must be editable afterwards as well:
- the reversible exterior side (DW-003);
- hinge side, opening side and sliding direction, each changeable independently (DW-004);
- the anchor (DW-005);
- pinned edge, reference (host base, host top or any storey, reassignable) and offset (DW-007);
- reveal face and offset, with core faces for layered walls (DW-008).
The defaults-versus-selection pattern exists in crates/archicraft-app/src/ui/properties.rs:34-52 (Subject::Defaults/Selection with prototype()), generalised by element-kind-framework-04 to DefaultsSlot prototypes (Project::prototype). components-dw-catalogue-11 provides the schema-driven parameter section (ui/parameters.rs parameter_fields), which hides parameters bound to OpeningWidth/OpeningHeight when the carrier owns them, and components-dw-catalogue-12 provides definition_picker.

WHAT: Extend ui/properties.rs, or the FieldSpec entries of the door and window descriptors if properties-and-dialogs has landed:
- Subject::Defaults(Door/Window) edits the kind's DefaultsSlot prototype, whose WallInsert has no host;
- placement fields: width, height, anchor (Left, Centre, Right as seen from the exterior), pinned edge, reference (Home storey, Host base, Host top or a storey list), offset with a 'keep elevation' checkbox, reveal face plus offset with a 'keep position' checkbox, a 'Flip exterior side' button, hinge end, swing side and sliding direction (each shown only for the matching leaf types), and the definition picker;
- core-face reveal options are offered only when every selected host is layered (DW-008 'for layered walls');
- parameter_fields for the instance parameters sits below them.
With nothing selected, edits write the tool defaults through Command::SetToolDefaults. With a selection, each field edit is one 02 command (ResizeInserts, SetInsertAnchor, FlipInsertSide, SetInsertConfiguration, SetInsertVertical, SetInsertReveal), so each is one undo step and the multi-selection rules apply. Fields hide when the getter returns None (existing Fields pattern), so mixed selections with walls show only the common fields. The settings dialog (properties.rs settings_dialog, line 770) shows the same fields in Grid layout. Out of scope: DW-015 derived read-only values (clear opening, key points; library-dw-exchange) and DW-017 convention settings (components-dw-catalogue-11).

WHY: DW-002: 'During placement the system shall show a live preview of the door or window and its void on the host under the pointer.' DW-003: the exterior side taken from the nearest face is 'shown in the preview'. DW-004: 'After the position click, the user shall choose the opening configuration with a preview: for hinged leaves, one of four combinations (hinge left/right x opening towards either side); for sliding leaves, the sliding direction.' The gap analysis notes that tool previews are polylines only (views/tools.rs:547) and that there is no second tool phase. point-input-snapping replaces them with the Preview of input/consumer.rs, and host-openings-10 adds Preview.meshes (PreviewMesh with PreviewStyle::Void and HostHighlight), drawn in plan and, through Renderer3d::draw_overlay, in 3D views.

WHAT: The insert tool (07) returns a Preview built from the same functions as the evaluated model: a throw-away Element with the WallInsert the click would create, run through InsertFrame::resolve (01), insert_void (03) and the instance evaluation of 04. The preview holds:
- the void as a PreviewMesh with PreviewStyle::Void;
- the unit's Simplified parts as meshes;
- the placed SymbolPrimitive list and an exterior-side marker (add a symbols field to Preview if it has none).
It is drawn in plan and in open 3D views through the overlay, and follows the hovered or cycled host, side and anchor live.

Configuration phase:
- after the position click on a type with hinged leaves, the pointer quadrant relative to the click point, measured in the wall frame, selects hinge end x swing side (four combinations), previewed live;
- for sliding leaves, the pointer side along the wall selects the direction;
- fixed and empty types skip the phase;
- a click confirms and issues one Command::AddElements, and Esc returns to the hover phase;
- the prompt shows the handedness label from handedness(project.settings.handedness, Handing::from_instance(..)) of components-dw-catalogue-03 (DIN 107 by default, Appendix D.6).
Out of scope: editing the configuration afterwards (09, 02).

WHY: Doors and windows are hosted wall elements whose position is defined along three independent axes (SRS 4.18). DW-001: they 'shall exist only in a host wall', take their storey association from the host, move with it and are deleted with it. DW-005: a horizontal anchor (left, centre, right) defines which point coincides with the pointer at placement and stays fixed when the width changes. DW-006: width and height are numeric parameters of the wall opening (Appendix D.2: door 0.90 x 2.10 m, window 1.20 x 1.40 m). DW-007: the vertical position is a pinned edge (sill or head), a reference (host wall base, host wall top or any storey) and an offset; resizing keeps the pinned edge fixed, and the reference storey may differ from the host's storey. DW-008: the position through the wall is a reveal offset from the wall's exterior face, interior face or, for layered walls, a core face. DW-010: a rectangular empty opening type (no frame, no leaf) uses the same placement, anchor and reveal rules. The gap analysis rates all of these 'missing': there is no Door or Window kind, no hosted placement data, and ToolDefaults (crates/archicraft-model/src/project.rs:146) holds only wall and slab defaults.

The prerequisites deliver everything below the insert itself:
- element-kind-framework: the KindDescriptor registry, per-kind DefaultsSlot tool defaults and the relation store;
- host-openings-01/02: Relation::Host derived from Kind::host(), the hosted flag, HostCapability, and the delete, move and copy cascade with Kind::translate and Kind::carry;
- layer-structures-02/03: CrossSection with core offsets, and ReferenceLine::distance_from_exterior;
- components-dw-catalogue-01/03: ComponentInstance, DefinitionRef, the OpeningWidth/OpeningHeight parameter bindings, and the built-in definitions door.hinged.single, window.tilt-turn and opening.rectangular.
This task adds only the insert data model and its resolver.

WHAT: New crates/archicraft-model/src/kinds/insert.rs with the shared placement WallInsert {host: ElementId, instance: ComponentInstance, along, anchor: Anchor, width, height, vertical: VerticalPin {edge: PinnedEdge, reference: VerticalReference, offset}, reveal: Reveal, exterior: WallSide, configuration: OpeningConfiguration}. The enums are listed in the design notes. The pure resolver InsertFrame::resolve(project, element) -> Result<InsertFrame, String> returns:
- the along-wall interval [s0, s1] and the anchor point;
- sill and head, as absolute elevations and relative to the host's home storey;
- the reveal distance of the unit's exterior frame face from the wall's exterior face;
- unit_to_storey: a proper transform (determinant > 0) from the catalogue's unit-local axes to storey coordinates;
- effective_instance(): the ComponentInstance with width and height written into the parameters bound to OpeningWidth/OpeningHeight, and mirrored plus the swing and opening-side parameters derived from configuration and exterior side.
insert.rs also provides along_for_pointer(host, point, anchor, width) for the tool.

kinds/door.rs and kinds/window.rs register Door(WallInsert) and Window(WallInsert) in the kinds! macro with:
- labels, codes door and window, and name prefixes;
- default classes IfcDoor and IfcWindow, with class sets [IfcDoor, IfcOpeningElement] and [IfcWindow, IfcOpeningElement] (variants from element-kind-framework-05);
- hosted: true, with walls as the only HostCapability, and Kind::host();
- component()/component_mut() returning the instance;
- a normalising validator (element.storey := host storey, instance fields re-derived);
- translate (a unit moved alone slides along its host by the projected delta) and carry (unchanged, so the unit follows any host transform rigidly);
- template_defaults slots from Appendix D.2.
Project::new_door and Project::new_window(host, along, &slot) take storey, layer and position (ELM-012) from the host, and the element class from the definition's default_class.

Stubs keep the registry completeness and module-layout tests green:
- crates/archicraft-geometry/src/kinds/door.rs and window.rs return empty geometry until tasks 03-04;
- crates/archicraft-ifcx/src/alpha/kinds/door.rs and window.rs are placeholder codecs until tasks 05-06.
The empty opening is the catalogue's opening.rectangular definition (traits.insertable_as door and window), bound to either kind, with class IfcOpeningElement.

Out of scope: edit commands and host/storey hooks (02), geometry (03, 04), IFCX (05, 06), UI (07-10), arc walls (DW-019, curved-geometry), roof windows (DW-021) and corner windows (DW-016).

design:
Follow the 'Add an element kind' extension point as rewritten by element-kind-framework: one module per kind in each crate, one registry line each, name counters keyed by kind.

Store only parameters, never derived values. 'along' plus anchor plus width gives the interval; edge, offset and reference give the elevations. The DW-005 and DW-007 invariants (anchor and pinned edge stay fixed on resize) then follow from the representation and need no fix-up code.

Store side-dependent choices in host-axis terms, so that the flip of DW-003 never moves geometry:
- Anchor {AlongStart, Centre, AlongEnd}, labelled left/right as seen from the unit's exterior side;
- WallSide {HostExterior, HostInterior};
- OpeningConfiguration {Fixed, Hinged {hinge: AlongStart|AlongEnd, swing: TowardsHostExterior|TowardsHostInterior}, Sliding {towards: AlongStart|AlongEnd}};
- Reveal {Face {face: RevealFace {Exterior, Interior, CoreExterior, CoreInterior}, offset}, Centred {within: Wall|Core}}, measured from the chosen wall face to the unit's nearer frame face.

Unit-local axes are those of components-dw-catalogue-04: x along the wall seen from the exterior, 0 at the left outer frame edge; y through the wall, 0 at the exterior frame face, +y to the interior; z up from the bottom of the opening. With exterior = HostExterior, x is the reference-line direction and y the left normal. With HostInterior both are negated, which is a rotation by 180 degrees about z. The flip is therefore a proper rotation, and handing is expressed only through instance.mirrored (IFX-051).

The configuration is authoritative. instance.mirrored, the swing and opening-side parameters, and the parameters bound to OpeningWidth/OpeningHeight are re-derived by the normalising validator, so they can never disagree with the placement.

Reuse existing code:
- ReferenceLine::face_offsets(&CrossSection) and ReferenceLine::distance_from_exterior (layer-structures-03; today face_offsets is at crates/archicraft-model/src/element.rs:99-107);
- CrossSection.core for CoreExterior and CoreInterior; a Thickness structure resolves to one core skin, so its core faces equal its wall faces;
- Project::top_elevation (project.rs:236) and StoreyList::get (storey.rs:84) for the references.

The validator runs through the descriptor in validate.rs. It requires finite values, width and height of at least MIN_HEIGHT (validate.rs:23), a host that exists and is a wall, and [s0, s1] inside [0, host length] within MODEL_TOLERANCE, with the ELM-017-style message 'The door does not fit in wall W-003'. Defaults-slot prototypes have no host, so check_tool_defaults validates only their placement values. Clear length against the junction faces is a geometry check (03).

The resolver is the single source of truth for geometry (03, 04), the IFCX transforms (05), the tool and its preview (07, 08) and the properties (09). Keep it free of geometry and UI dependencies (NFR-MNT-002/004).

Pitfall: the wall's ReferenceLine docs (element.rs:83-85) put the exterior on the right of the start-to-end direction, and the local frame must use the same convention.

notes:
SRS: DW-001 DW-005 DW-006 DW-007 DW-008 DW-010. Appendix D.2: door 0.90 x 2.10 m single-leaf hinged, sill at the storey elevation, centred in the wall; window 1.20 x 1.40 m tilt-and-turn single sash, sill 0.90 m above the storey elevation, centred in the core. F2 core faces (Appendix D.7): 120 + 60 + 180 + 10 = 370 mm and 370 + 300 = 670 mm from the exterior face.

Design decisions to record in docs/components.md:
- VerticalReference::HomeStorey follows the host's storey, so the D.2 sill reference survives rehoming;
- Reveal::Centred{Wall|Core} serves the D.2 'centred' defaults, since DW-008 names only face offsets;
- the empty opening is a definition, not a third kind;
- the window name prefix must not collide with the wall prefix 'W' (element.rs:221, ELM-019); proposal 'WI'.
IfcClass::Door, Window and OpeningElement come from element-kind-framework-05, so no class is added here.

description:
WHY: The tool is where DW-001's host rule meets the user: 'Without a valid host under the pointer, the tool shall give clear feedback and create nothing.' DW-002: 'where several hosts are candidates (junctions, overlaps), a single key shall cycle through them'. DW-003 takes the exterior side from the host face nearest the pointer at the position click. DW-005: the anchor point coincides with the pointer. DW-010 places the empty opening with the same tool rules. The increment-1 exit criterion asks for a ground floor with openings drawn precisely, so placement must accept snapped and typed distances along the wall. Appendix D.8 requires shortcuts for the door and window tools. Today the tool state machine (crates/archicraft-app/src/views/tools.rs) has only select, wall and slab tools, previews are polylines, and Tab cycling exists only for the select tool (views/plan.rs:450-458). The prerequisites change this:
- point-input-snapping moves the tools into crates/archicraft-app/src/tools/ as InputConsumers on the PointInput service, and uses Tab for snap-candidate cycling (INP-009) and field switching;
- host-openings-10 provides the host candidate list (input/host.rs candidates_in_plan) and the host-highlight overlay, but leaves the host-cycling key to this epic;
- components-dw-catalogue-12 provides ui/catalogue.rs definition_picker.

WHAT: New crates/archicraft-app/src/tools/insert.rs with InsertTool implementing InputConsumer (input/consumer.rs), next to the opening tool of host-openings-11, for ToolKind::Door and ToolKind::Window. The definition is chosen through definition_picker, filtered to the kind's category (door or window) plus the definitions whose insertable_as includes it (opening.rectangular and the other empty openings).
- Hover phase: candidates_in_plan, filtered by the door/window HostCapability (walls only, without locked or hidden walls, Project::is_editable), ordered by distance to the reference line. A new Action::CycleHost, a dedicated key distinct from Tab, steps through them; the active candidate gets the overlay host highlight (PreviewStyle::HostHighlight) and an info label with its name (SEL-001 style).
- With no candidate, the StepSpec prompt says 'Doors and windows need a wall: move the pointer over a wall'. A click calls Workspace::error with the same text (workspace.rs:111), returns Outcome::Nothing and creates nothing.
- Position click: the resolved point (snap, constraint or typed value) projects onto the host's reference line, and a typed distance is measured from the snapped reference point (wall end node or junction face). along_for_pointer (01) turns the point and the anchor into 'along', and the nearer host face sets 'exterior'.
- Types without leaves (fixed windows, empty openings) emit Command::AddElements(vec![project.new_door/new_window(..)]) at once. Leaf types enter the configuration phase of task 08, with the slot's configuration as a temporary default until 08 lands.
Wiring:
- Action::ToolDoor, Action::ToolWindow and Action::CycleHost in ui/actions.rs, with free keys; appendix_d8_commands_have_shortcuts gains the two tools;
- run() arms in app.rs (app.rs:377);
- toolbox buttons and Design menu items (ui/shell.rs);
- the ToolKind-to-KindTag mapping in gather() (ui/properties.rs:55);
- select_all (workspace.rs:204).
If candidate_in_3d exists, the same consumer runs in 3D views through the Camera3d ViewSurface; the plan path is the acceptance. Out of scope: preview geometry and configuration choice (08), properties fields (09).

WHY: DW-018: 'Doors and windows shall be written to IFCX as IfcDoor / IfcWindow occurrences with a Void child and material parts (IFX-036); with IFX-050 and IFX-051, as occurrences of shared types.' Table 5-1 puts doors and windows below their host wall, with a Void and with parts via the type. IFX-036 makes the Void an invisible closed mesh child of the occurrence, not of the type. IFX-050 keeps host-independent parts in a shared type node referenced by inherits, and IFX-051 forbids negative-determinant transforms, so mirrored units reference a mirrored type. Under IFX-025, the empty opening of DW-010 is written as IfcOpeningElement with a Void. Today writer.rs writes only Body and Axis children (crates/archicraft-ifcx/src/alpha/writer.rs:361-406) and has no door or window classes. The prerequisites supply the mechanisms:
- hosted children through the contained_children hook, with an invisible Void (host-openings-08);
- archicraft::component, embedded definitions, type nodes, occurrence inherits, the mirrored TypeKey and the positive-determinant guard (components-dw-catalogue-08/09);
- KindCodec and the migration table (element-kind-framework-06/12).
This task writes doors and windows with them.

WHAT: crates/archicraft-ifcx/src/alpha/kinds/door.rs and window.rs replace the placeholder codecs of 01 with KindCodec implementations for archicraft::door and archicraft::window. They write the placement only:
- host, along, anchor, width and height;
- vertical {edge, reference kind, storey reference, offset};
- reveal {kind, face, within, offset};
- exterior and configuration {kind, hinge, swing, towards};
- the slot codec for the tool defaults.
The definition and the instance parameters travel in archicraft::component. The schema declarations come from the model's ALL arrays. door and window enter the kind enumeration through CODECS, and FORMAT_VERSION is bumped with an identity entry in MIGRATIONS (IFX-062).

writer.rs writes each insert through contained_children as a child of its host wall node, with child keys unique per parent (child_key, writer.rs:56). Each child carries:
- bsi::ifc::class IfcDoor, IfcWindow or IfcOpeningElement from the element's class;
- bsi::ifc::prop::Name, and IsExternal through the existing position path (writer.rs:399-401), with the position copied from the host by 01;
- usd::xformop.transform = InstanceGeometry.transform, row-major (IFX-031); the wall node is untransformed below the storey;
- a Void child with id sub_node_id(occurrence, 'Void'): an occurrence-local mesh with usd::usdgeom::visibility invisible and no material (IFX-036);
- inherits.type to the catalogue's type node, written by types.rs (components-dw-catalogue-09), so identical units share one type and mesh set, and a mirrored unit references a separate mirrored type (IFX-050, IFX-051, IFX-037).
Definitions with traits.has_parts == false (the empty openings) write no type node and no inherits.

The writer tests run on a project built in the test. samples() in tests/roundtrip.rs is extended in task 06 together with the reader, so the round-trip tests stay green. Out of scope: the reader (06), third-party door import (ifcx-interop).

