description:
WHY: Appendix D.7 F9: 'Two-storey family house on a sloping site with storeys -3, -2, -1, 0, +1, stepped footings, single-slope roof and a flat terrace with falls (source of the site, footing and terrace details of RM-S; AS-01 is a simplified three-storey variant with a gable roof)'. terrain-and-site-17 already built samples::f9_sloping_site() through the FixtureBuilder and registered it in the catalogue with the golden f9-sloping-site.ifcx: storeys -3 (-8.40), -2 (-5.60), -1 (-2.80), 0 (+-0.00) and +1 (+2.80), a 40 x 30 m terrain from 36 spot elevations and 4 breaklines, base slabs cutting the terrain, stepped strip footings and a terrace slab with falls. It left the single-slope roof and the D.7 registry entry to this epic. The epic acceptance requires that F3 (floating floor +6.10 / +6.00 / +5.75, base offset -0.10, resilient layer) and F9 pass, and that the conformance job runs on every registered fixture: NFR-MNT-003, 'automated conformance tests against the buildingSMART reference implementation (IFX-006)'. F3 was built and registered by layer-structures-09. This task re-runs it on the complete increment-2 workspace (slab cleanup and trims now active).

WHAT: Extend samples::f9_sloping_site(); do not rebuild it:
- add the single-plane roof (ROF-001, roofs) over storey +1 with a typed pitch, with the top walls trimmed to it through Command::TrimElements or given a fixed height;
- keep the storey table, terrain, footings and terrace of terrain-and-site-17, which since task 01 go through the shared step functions in samples/site.rs;
- re-bless f9-sloping-site.ifcx with ARCHICRAFT_BLESS and a reviewed diff limited to the roof and trims.
Register F9 in the fixture registry crates/archicraft-model/src/fixtures.rs (the regression-and-status-02 entry shape `Fixture { id, title, source, owner, increment, seeded, build }`, created by layer-structures-09 if regression-and-status has not landed): the D7 entry F9 gets build: Some(fixtures::f9_sloping_site) delegating to the samples constructor, and seeded: true. F9-specific D.7 assertions go into the registry test crates/archicraft-model/tests/fixtures.rs and into crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/fixtures_f9.rs; the generic registry checks (crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/fixtures.rs, crates/archicraft-ifcx/tests/fixtures.rs) cover F9 automatically. Raise fixtures::REQUIRED_INCREMENT to 2 only if every D7 entry with increment 2 or lower has a constructor; F8 belongs to curved-geometry, which is not a prerequisite of this epic, so otherwise leave the gate and file a bead naming F8's owner. Out of scope: writing the fixture files for the conformance job (04) and RM-S itself (01, 02, 10).

acceptance_criteria:
- The re-saved file validates with archicraft_ifcx::validate without problems (IFX-006 a, offline), and conformance/run.sh is green on rm-s-as03.ifcx in CI (IFX-006 a and the tree of b).
- UUIDs: the element, storey, resource and relation id sets before and after are equal. For every sub-node role present before (Body, Skin_n, Void, Axis, Part_n, Flight_n, Landing_n), the v5 id is unchanged. Any role that appears or disappears fails the test with its path.
- Parametric: the reload reports no problems; every element keeps its kind and parametric data (none became a free-form solid or a mesh); the Host, Trim and Junction relation sets are equal to before.
- Junctions follow:
  - exterior-face corners are bit-identical;
  - the inner corner of every L between exterior walls moves 0.05 m outward (towards the fixed exterior face) along both axes, and L corners between interior walls are unchanged;
  - every interior wall abutting an exterior wall now ends on the new interior face (its body is 0.05 m longer per abutting end);
  - the node-envelope coverage helper still passes.
- Openings follow: every void in an exterior wall has the new depth of 0.630 m, and voids in interior walls are unchanged; along, width, height and sill are unchanged; each host's skin volumes equal the gross volume minus the voids.
- Slabs follow:
  - the attic slab's extent shifts by +0.30 m, so the F3 relation holds at +6.40 / +6.30 / +6.05;
  - the 00 and 01 slab extents are unchanged;
  - the slab-wall cleanup leaves no overlap (zero intersection volume).
- Roof follows: pivot, eaves and ridge rise by 0.30 m; every trimmed gable and knee wall top lies on the roof underside within MODEL_TOLERANCE; the 01-02 stair is re-solved for the 3.40 m rise within the D.3 rules without an STA-011 report, and no part of it intersects the 02 slab; the 00-01 stair is unchanged.
- Undoing both edits and saving reproduces the golden bytes (undo is exact on the real model).
- Satisfies the automated AS-03 scenario on RM-S and the NFR-MNT-003 conformance check of the re-saved file.

description:
WHY: NFR-USE-006 (S, demonstration): 'Defaults (templates, element settings, materials, layer structures) shall let a new user model the AS-01 house without first creating any material, layer structure or other attribute.' The gap row is partial. The template has 25 materials with D.4 priorities, 11 layers, two storeys and the D.2 wall/slab defaults (crates/archicraft-model/src/template.rs:28-343), but layer structures, presets and the D.2 defaults for doors, windows, roofs, stairs, railings, columns, beams, terrain and grids were missing. The prerequisite epics have added them since. The epic acceptance asks for 'a test asserts the RM-S builder uses only template attributes (NFR-USE-006 demonstration script under docs/)'. Tasks 01, 02 and 10 already check the builder's command record inline. This task makes the check exhaustive and future-proof, and writes the demonstration.

WHAT:
- In crates/archicraft-model/src/command.rs, add `Command::attribute_effect(&self) -> AttributeEffect { None, Creates, Changes, Deletes }` as an exhaustive match over every variant. Reuse the category metadata if the command registry of commands-and-l10n already carries one.
- Read the builder's complete command record, FixtureBuilder::commands() of task 01. History::journal() is no substitute: it keeps labels only and at most 200 entries.
- Add crates/archicraft-model/tests/nfr_use_006.rs with rm_s_uses_only_template_attributes:
  - no command in commands() has an attribute effect;
  - every attribute collection of rm_s() equals that of FixtureBuilder::new("rm_s").finish();
  - every element reference (material, layer, structure, profile, component definition, stair rule set) resolves to a template resource;
  - element settings: for every element kind in rm_s(), the template's tool defaults exist and reference only template resources;
  - replaying commands() with archicraft_model::replay on the seeded template reproduces rm_s();
  - it prints a usage table (template attribute -> number of elements).
- docs/test-plan/nfr-use-006-defaults-demonstration.md: the protocol for a user new to ArchiCraft who follows the AS-01 procedure without the railing, an observer sheet that records every attribute created or edited (pass = none), the usage table, and one recorded run.
If the builder needed something the template lacks, extend template.rs, never the builder, and re-bless the goldens with a reviewed diff. Out of scope: presets and templates as files (templates-presets) and the railing defaults (railings-stairs-adv).

WHY: AS-01 needs a 'gable roof with the gable walls trimmed to the roof', and AS-03 later requires that 'the roof follow[s] the change'. SRS 7.3 sets RM-S at about 300 elements, which matters for its roles as functional regression model, golden IFCX file and minimum-hardware performance model. NFR-MNT-003 needs the complete model before task 04 takes the golden file. NFR-USE-006 requires the roof and members to come from the D.2 defaults (roof pitch 35 degrees, 0.30 m thick, eaves overhang 0.50 m, pivot at the top of the walls; column 0.30 x 0.30 m and beam 0.25 x 0.50 m reinforced concrete) and template materials, never from new attributes. Appendix D.7 names F9 as the source of RM-S's terrace details.

WHAT: In samples/rm_s.rs, add the stage `rm_s_roof_and_members` and complete rm_s():
- the gable roof over the attic, generated from the knee walls in wall-outline mode (roofs-07) with the D.2 roof (roofs);
- the gable and knee walls trimmed to the roof through Command::TrimElements: Relation::Trim with KeepSide::Inside of the roof's default Downward trimming volume (cleanup-and-trims-05, SOL-030);
- roof windows in the roof planes (roofs-12, DW-021) if they have landed;
- members that bring the model to SRS 7.3's size: a terrace canopy of columns and beams with the D.2 defaults, timber purlins and a ridge beam (template material Softwood);
- the terrace slab with falls (SlabSlope of layer-structures-07, the same representation as F9's terrace) and garden steps on the slope.
The module doc records the per-storey element budget. Geometry assertions extend crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/rm_s.rs. Out of scope: the railing (railings-stairs-adv), the curtain-wall entrance (perf-tuning-rm-m-l), objects and furniture (library-dw-exchange), and the golden file (04).

WHY: AS-01 passes when 'the file passes IFX-006. In the IFC5 viewer, the tree shows project/site/building/3 storeys. Walls show openings and clean L/T junctions. Materials and colours are as modelled.' IFX-006 (b) requires the spatial tree to be verified by automated tests on the reference models. NFR-MNT-003 wants automated conformance tests on every change. Today check.ts (conformance/check.ts:68-105) only checks a generic tree, bodies, triangle lists and that a colour exists. It cannot tell three storeys from two, a wall with a void from one without, or the right colour from any colour. The epic acceptance asks that 'conformance/check.ts asserts the AS-01 criteria (tree project/site/building/3 storeys, openings in walls, clean junctions, modelled colours against the material table)'.

WHAT: Extend the expectations sidecar that grids-11 introduced (<golden>.expect.json, read by check.ts when present) with an optional "as01" block. Commit crates/archicraft-ifcx/tests/golden/rm-s.expect.json with:
- the storey names in order (exactly three) and the site children (one IfcGeographicElement with a body);
- class counts (IfcWall, IfcFooting, IfcSlab, IfcDoor, IfcWindow, IfcStair, IfcRoof, IfcBeam, IfcColumn);
- the junction list: wall names, node point and the corner points of every L and T, computed by hand from the rm_s.rs layout constants;
- the expected set of material names.
check.ts gains these assertions, with the shared geometry in conformance/geometry.ts (transform accumulation down the composed tree, Moller-Trumbore ray-triangle, ray-parity point-in-mesh, signed volume):
- tree: the exact storey list under the single building;
- openings: every IfcDoor, IfcWindow and IfcOpeningElement under an IfcWall has an invisible closed Void child, and a ray through the void's centre along the host's thickness direction hits no host skin triangle inside the void depth;
- junctions: at every listed node, no vertex of one wall's meshes lies strictly inside another wall's closed mesh (overlap), and each listed corner point is a vertex of at least one of the walls (no gap);
- colours: every visible mesh's diffuseColor and opacity equal, within 1/255, the colour of the material it inherits (inherits.material, the material node written at writer.rs:329-336), compared with the entry of the same id in the archicraft::project material table of the same file.
A self-test (an npm test script in conformance/package.json that bundles conformance/test/selftest.ts with esbuild and runs it with node, so no test framework is added) runs check.ts on mutated in-memory copies of rm-s.ifcx and expects each to fail. The ci.yml conformance job runs it after run.sh, so it runs on every push. A Rust test keeps the sidecar honest against the model. Out of scope: the Playwright viewer run itself (regression-and-status) and the AS-03 file (06).

acceptance_criteria:
- conformance/run.sh prints PASS for rm-s.ifcx with a summary that names 3 storeys and the numbers of openings, junctions and coloured meshes checked.
- npm test in conformance/ runs these mutations and each one FAILs with a message naming the offender:
  - drop the attic storey: the message names the storey list;
  - remove a door's Void child: it names the door;
  - translate one exterior wall's meshes 5 mm into its neighbour: it names the junction;
  - change one material colour in the table: it names the material and the mesh;
  - delete the terrain: it names IfcSite.
- The ci.yml conformance job runs npm test in conformance/ after run.sh on every push and fails the job on a self-test failure.
- Files without an "as01" block (walls-and-slab, two-storey-shell, fixtures) behave exactly as before.
- rm_s_sidecar_matches_the_model (crates/archicraft-ifcx/tests/roundtrip.rs):
  - the storey names, class counts and junction wall pairs of rm-s.expect.json equal those derived from samples::rm_s() and its junction graph;
  - every sidecar corner point equals the evaluated footprint corner within MODEL_TOLERANCE, so neither side can drift silently.
- If the Playwright viewer job of regression-and-status exists, it reads the same storey list from the sidecar.
- Satisfies the automated part of the AS-01 pass criteria (IFX-006 a and the tree of b, openings, junctions, colours) for NFR-MNT-003. The visual viewer check stays manual, once per release (IFX-006).

description:
WHY: The epic acceptance ends with 'docs/implementation-status.md reflects the increment-2 state'. Appendix F sets the increment-2 exit criterion as 'AS-01 without railings and AS-02 pass'. The current document (generated 2026-10-17) shows increment 2 as '92 in scope: 0/0/92', AS-01 and AS-02 as not-yet-possible, AS-03 as partial, 'RM-S, RM-M, RM-L and RX-1...n are all absent' and 'F3 partial, F1, F2, F4-F9 absent'. The rows of NFR-MNT-003 (golden files and conformance on the reference models) and NFR-USE-006 (defaults suffice for AS-01) change status with this epic. The status tooling of regression-and-status-19 keeps the rows in docs/status/requirements.json, regenerates the tables with scripts/status.py and compares each merged epic's srs_ids with the changed rows (merge-check).

WHAT: Update the rows of NFR-MNT-003 and NFR-USE-006 in docs/status/requirements.json, regenerate the tables with `python3 scripts/status.py generate` and verify with `check` and `merge-check`. Then rewrite the narrative sections by hand:
- the Summary;
- the increment-2 readiness row: the exit criterion with evidence for AS-01 without railing (rm-s golden, the check.ts as01 block, docs/test-plan/as-01-two-storey-house.md) and AS-02 (structural-frame golden, as02.rs);
- the acceptance-scenario rows AS-01, AS-02 and AS-03 (as03.rs, rm-s-as03.ifcx in conformance, the kittest, the AS-03 procedure);
- the reference-models line: RM-S present; the railing variant goes to railings-stairs-adv, RM-M and RM-L to perf-tuning-rm-m-l;
- the D.7 fixtures line: registered fixtures, and the owners of the rest;
- the technical-debt list, with items fixed by increments 1 and 2 removed only after a re-check in the code.
`check` already verifies that cited evidence paths exist (line numbers are deliberately not checked, they drift). Extend it, if it does not do so yet, so that a test cited as 'fn_name (path)' must exist in that file. If regression-and-status-19 has not landed, edit the rows and counts of docs/implementation-status.md by hand in its existing format, normalise the absolute worktree paths of the touched rows to repository-relative ones, and file a bead for the missing per-merge check. Out of scope: re-verifying other epics' requirement rows beyond the evidence check (their done tasks own them).

WHY: SRS 7.3 defines RM-S as the single-family house of AS-01 with about 300 elements, used for functional regression, the golden IFCX file and minimum-hardware performance; 'the reference models are built with ArchiCraft and kept as test assets in the repository. Their IFCX output is the golden file for NFR-MNT-003'. NFR-MNT-003 (M) asks for 'golden-file tests for IFCX export of the reference models, plus automated conformance tests against the buildingSMART reference implementation (IFX-006)' that 'run on every change'. Its gap row is partial: the golden and conformance inputs are two increment-0 samples of 5 and 12 elements (crates/archicraft-model/src/samples.rs:31-83) and RM-S does not exist. AS-01 starts with 'New project from the default template', and NFR-USE-006 (S) requires that the defaults (templates, element settings, materials, layer structures) 'let a new user model the AS-01 house without first creating any material, layer structure or other attribute'. The builder therefore starts from the template and only picks from it. Appendix D.7 names F9 as 'the source of the site, footing and terrace details of RM-S' and F3 as the floating-floor relation (finished floor +6.10 m, slab top +6.00 m, soffit +5.75 m, wall base offset -0.10 m, resilient layer 20-30 mm). The prerequisite epics deliver the ingredients: template layer structures and per-skin bodies (layer-structures, through junction-engine), the priority junction engine (junction-engine), terrain from spot elevations with site containment and the F9 site (terrain-and-site), the F9 sloping-site fixture samples::f9_sloping_site() with its stepped strip footings (terrain-and-site-17), footing class overrides with predefined types (element-kind-framework-05) and the slab-wall priority cleanup (cleanup-and-trims).

WHAT: If samples is still a single file, turn crates/archicraft-model/src/samples.rs into samples/mod.rs plus samples/rm_s.rs and samples/site.rs (public API unchanged). Add `pub fn rm_s() -> Project`, built with the FixtureBuilder of element-kind-framework-03 under the fixture name "rm_s" (v5 ids for every template resource, storey, element and relation) and through commands only.

Give FixtureBuilder a complete record of what it executed, `commands() -> &[Command]`, kept by the builder itself. Its History cannot serve: it keeps at most DEFAULT_UNDO_LIMIT = 200 transactions, and JournalEntry holds only labels, capped at JOURNAL_LIMIT = 200 (crates/archicraft-model/src/command.rs). A build of about 300 elements can exceed both.

This task builds the first stages as public step functions that tasks 02 and 10 continue:
- shared site code: promote the terrain and stepped strip-footing construction of samples::f9_sloping_site() (terrain-and-site-17) into public step functions in samples/site.rs that take typed tables (spot elevations and breaklines; footing runs with step elevations). f9_sloping_site() calls them with its own tables, and its golden f9-sloping-site.ifcx stays byte-identical;
- site: RM-S calls the terrain step function with its own typed spot-elevation table (a gentler fall than F9, see notes) and F9's project location. The terrain is created through the terrain commands and contained by the site;
- storeys: the template's '00 Ground floor' at +-0.00 and '01 First floor' at +3.00; 01's height set to 3.10 (Command::SetStoreyHeight) and '02 Attic' inserted above it at +6.10 (Command::InsertStorey);
- foundations: stepped strip footings from the shared footing step function (slabs with the IfcFooting class override and STRIP_FOOTING, as in F9) under the exterior and bearing interior walls, stepping with the slope; plinth walls (single-material, template material Concrete) from the footing tops up to the ground-slab soffit where the terrain falls away; the ground-floor slab ('Floating floor 350');
- shell: exterior walls with the template structure 'Cavity wall 680' (reference line on the exterior face) on 00 and 01; knee walls and gable walls of fixed height on 02 (trimmed in task 10); interior walls with 'Plastered masonry 135' that form L and T junctions; floor slabs with 'Floating floor 350' referenced to CoreTop at -0.10, with the stair openings on 01 and 02; wall base offset -0.10 on every storey, so the attic reproduces F3's absolute values.

Tests go into crates/archicraft-model/tests/rm_s.rs and crates/archicraft-geometry/tests/rm_s.rs. Out of scope: doors, windows and stairs (task 02); the roof, trims, members and terrace (task 10); catalogue registration and the golden file (04); the F9 roof and registry entry (03); check.ts (05). rm_s() is not yet in samples::catalogue(), so no golden changes.
